The nonce is random, so not reused. The real vulnerability is simpler: by logging in with an arbitrary username, the app gives us a valid GCM ciphertext and tag for plaintext `username|regular|FLAG`. If we can recover the GCM keystream for those blocks, we can decrypt the flag. Since GCM uses CTR, if we know some plaintext bytes we know keystream bytes there. We know the prefix `username|regular|`. By choosing a very long username, we can shift the unknown flag to later bytes while learning keystream over a large prefix of the ciphertext. Let's inspect `Cargo.toml` / dependency versions only if needed; for now I want to see whether the flag length is likely short enough that we can cover it by choosing username lengths that align blocks and then exploit the admin parser's rotation check with a crafted 3-part cookie. First, I want the full lower part of `main.rs` to see all routes one last time.
